                </div>
                <div class="kpi-card">
                    <div class="kpi-label">Maximum MoM Change</div>
                    <div class="kpi-value">{max_mom_pct:.1f}%</div>
                    <div style="font-size:0.8rem;color:#718096;">{df['Date'].iloc[max_mom_idx].strftime('%b %Y')}</div>
                </div>
                <div class="kpi-card">
                    <div class="kpi-label">Price Volatility (Std Dev)</div>
                    <div class="kpi-value">${std_dev:.2f}</div>
                    <div style="font-size:0.8rem;color:#718096;">Overall dispersion</div>
                </div>
                <div class="kpi-card highlight">
                    <div class="kpi-label">Highest Avg Decade</div>
                    <div class="kpi-value">{highest_avg_decade}s</div>
                    <div style="font-size:0.8rem;color:#718096;">Avg: ${decade_stats[decade_stats['Decade']==highest_avg_decade]['mean'].values[0]:.2f}/bbl</div>
                </div>
            </div>
        </section>

        <!-- Long-Term Trend -->
        <section class="section">
            <h2>Long-Term Price Trajectory (1970–2026)</h2>
            <p>The long-term trajectory of crude oil prices reveals several distinct regimes, each characterized by unique supply-demand dynamics and geopolitical influences. The chart below provides the complete time series visualization.</p>

            <div class="chart-container">
                <img src="data:image/png;base64,{chart1_b64}" alt="Long-term price trajectory">
            </div>

            <h3>Key Inflection Points</h3>
            <div class="timeline">
                <div class="timeline-item">
                    <div class="timeline-date">1973–1974: First Oil Shock</div>
                    <div class="timeline-content">OPEC embargo triggered prices to surge from ~$3 to $13/barrel—a 4x increase in months.</div>
                </div>
                <div class="timeline-item">
                    <div class="timeline-date">1979–1980: Iranian Revolution</div>
                    <div class="timeline-content">Political upheaval led to a second major shock, with prices spiking to ~$40/barrel.</div>
                </div>
                <div class="timeline-item">
                    <div class="timeline-date">1986: Price Collapse</div>
                    <div class="timeline-content">Saudi Arabia's decision to abandon price controls triggered a crash to $12–$15/barrel.</div>
                </div>
                <div class="timeline-item">
                    <div class="timeline-date">1990–1991: Gulf War</div>
                    <div class="timeline-content">Iraq's invasion of Kuwait caused a brief but sharp spike to $34/barrel.</div>
                </div>
                <div class="timeline-item">
                    <div class="timeline-date">2008: Peak and Financial Crisis</div>
                    <div class="timeline-content">Prices reached an all-time high of $132.83/barrel before collapsing 75% during the global financial crisis.</div>
                </div>
                <div class="timeline-item">
                    <div class="timeline-date">2014–2016: Shale Revolution</div>
                    <div class="timeline-content">US shale production surge drove prices from $100+ to sub-$30 levels.</div>
                </div>
                <div class="timeline-item">
                    <div class="timeline-date">2020: COVID-19 Shock</div>
                    <div class="timeline-content">Pandemic demand destruction drove prices to $21.04/barrel in April 2020.</div>
                </div>
                <div class="timeline-item">
                    <div class="timeline-date">2022: Post-Pandemic Recovery</div>
                    <div class="timeline-content">Supply constraints and energy transition pressures pushed prices above $110/barrel.</div>
                </div>
            </div>
        </section>

        <!-- Decade-by-Decade -->
        <section class="section">
            <h2>Decade-by-Decade Breakdown</h2>
            <p>Each decade presents a distinct pricing regime, reflecting different geopolitical contexts and market structures.</p>

            <div class="chart-container">
                <img src="data:image/png;base64,{chart2_b64}" alt="Decade comparison">
            </div>

            {decade_html}

            <h3>1970s: The Age of Shocks</h3>
            <p>The 1970s began with stable, low prices around $1.21–$1.87/barrel but ended with prices 30x higher. Two major shocks—the 1973 OPEC embargo and the 1979 Iranian revolution—fundamentally altered the global energy landscape and established oil as a strategic commodity.</p>

            <h3>1980s: Volatility and Collapse</h3>
            <p>The decade opened with prices near $40/barrel following the Iranian shock but soon entered a prolonged decline. The 1986 price collapse (triggered by Saudi Arabia's strategic shift) brought prices below $12/barrel, marking one of the steepest declines in oil history. The latter half of the decade saw gradual stabilization in the $15–$20 range.</p>

            <h3>1990s: Relative Stability</h3>
            <p>Unlike previous decades, the 1990s were characterized by moderate price levels ($15–$25/barrel) and lower volatility. The Gulf War spike of 1990 was brief. The latter part of the decade saw the Asian financial crisis reduce demand, pushing prices toward decade lows by 1998.</p>

            <h3>2000s: The Super-Cycle</h3>
            <p>The 2000s witnessed a dramatic super-cycle driven by rapid Chinese industrialization and global economic growth. Prices crossed $100/barrel for the first time in 2008, reaching an all-time nominal high of $132.83 in July 2008 before the financial crisis caused a violent reversal.</p>

            <h3>2010s: The Shale Era</h3>
            <p>The decade began with elevated prices but US shale production surge fundamentally changed global supply dynamics. By 2016, prices had retreated to decade lows near $26/barrel. The latter half of the decade saw moderate recovery to $50–$75 range despite trade tensions and demand concerns.</p>

            <h3>2020s: Pandemic and Recovery</h3>
            <p>The 2020s brought unprecedented volatility: COVID-19 crashed prices to $21.04/barrel in April 2020, the fastest demand destruction on record. The subsequent recovery was equally dramatic, with prices rebounding above $100/barrel in 2022 as supply chain disruptions collided with energy transition pressures. The 2020s average (${decade_stats[decade_stats['Decade']==2020]['mean'].values[0]:.2f}/barrel) remains below the 2000s peak.</p>
        </section>

        <!-- Volatility and Shocks -->
        <section class="section">
            <h2>Price Volatility & Major Shocks</h2>
            <p>Crude oil is renowned for its price volatility, driven by the intersection of geopolitical events, supply disruptions, and macroeconomic cycles. Below we quantify the most significant price movements.</p>

            <div class="chart-container">
                <img src="data:image/png;base64,{chart3_b64}" alt="Price volatility">
            </div>

            <h3>Year-over-Year Price Changes</h3>
            <div class="chart-container">
                <img src="data:image/png;base64,{chart4_b64}" alt="YoY changes">
            </div>

            <h3>Major Shocks (YoY Change > 50%)</h3>
            {shocks_html}

            <h3>Historical Context of Major Events</h3>
            <div class="highlight-event">
                <strong>1973 OPEC Embargo:</strong> Arab members of OPEC proclaimed an oil embargo against the US and other nations that supported Israel during the Yom Kippur War. Prices surged from ~$3 to $13/barrel within months.
            </div>
            <div class="highlight-event">
                <strong>1979 Iranian Revolution:</strong> The overthrow of the Shah led to a 50% drop in Iran's oil exports, triggering prices to spike from ~$15 to $40/barrel.
            </div>
            <div class="highlight-event">
                <strong>1986 Saudi Price War:</strong> In response to non-OPEC production growth, Saudi Arabia abandoned its swing producer role and flooded the market, causing prices to collapse by over 50%.
            </div>
            <div class="highlight-event">
                <strong>1990 Gulf War:</strong> Iraq's invasion of Kuwait removed ~4.5 million barrels/day from the market, spiking prices from $15 to $34/barrel within weeks.
            </div>
            <div class="highlight-event">
                <strong>2008 Financial Crisis:</strong> The collapse of Lehman Brothers triggered a global demand shock, with prices falling from $132 to $33/barrel in six months (-75%).
            </div>
            <div class="highlight-event">
                <strong>2020 COVID-19:</strong> Global lockdowns caused demand to plummet by ~30%, with storage constraints pushing prices into negative territory in April 2020 for WTI futures.
            </div>
        </section>

        <!-- Statistical Summary -->
        <section class="section">
            <h2>Statistical Summary & Distribution</h2>
            <p>The following statistics characterize the full distribution of monthly crude oil prices across the 56-year dataset.</p>

            <div class="chart-container">
                <img src="data:image/png;base64,{chart5_b64}" alt="Price distribution">
            </div>

            {stats_html}

            <h3>Distribution Insights</h3>
            <p>The price distribution is strongly right-skewed, with a mean (${mean_price:.2f}) considerably higher than the median (${median_price:.2f}). This reflects the influence of extreme high-price episodes (particularly in 2008 and 2022) that pull the average upward while most observations cluster at lower price levels.</p>
            <p>The interquartile range (${percentile_25:.2f}–${percentile_75:.2f}) captures the middle 50% of observations, suggesting that for half of the dataset's history, prices fell between approximately $17 and $71 per barrel.</p>
        </section>

        <!-- Recent Trend (2020s) -->
        <section class="section">
            <h2>2020s: The Decade So Far</h2>
            <div class="chart-container">
                <img src="data:image/png;base64,{chart6_b64}" alt="2020s trend">
            </div>
            <p>The 2020s began with prices around $60/barrel before the COVID-19 pandemic caused the fastest demand destruction in history. April 2020 saw prices bottom at $21.04/barrel. The recovery was asymmetric: a swift initial rebound driven by supply constraints, followed by volatile sideways movement as geopolitical tensions (Russia-Ukraine) and monetary policy shifts (interest rate hikes) alternately pressured prices.</p>
            <p>As of March 2026, prices stand at $87.84/barrel, representing a partial recovery but remaining below the 2022 highs above $110/barrel.</p>
        </section>

        <!-- Outlook -->
        <section class="section">
            <h2>Outlook & Conclusions</h2>
            <h3>Key Takeaways</h3>
            <ol style="margin: 15px 0 15px 20px;">
                <li><strong>Long-term secular growth:</strong> Despite dramatic shorter-term volatility, crude oil prices have exhibited a positive long-term trend, rising at a CAGR of {cagr:.2f}% from 1970 to 2026.</li>
                <li><strong>Structural regime changes:</strong> The market has undergone fundamental shifts—from OPEC dominance (1970s) to non-OPEC competition (1980s–90s) to US shale as swing producer (2010s).</li>
                <li><strong>Volatility as a constant:</strong> Standard deviation of ${std_dev:.2f} underscores that oil remains a highly volatile commodity, with month-over-month swings frequently exceeding 20%.</li>
                <li><strong>Geopolitical sensitivity:</strong> Major price events correlate strongly with geopolitical disruptions, underscoring oil's continued strategic importance.</li>
            </ol>

            <h3>Recent Trends and Forward Outlook</h3>
            <p>The post-pandemic period has been characterized by elevated volatility and structurally higher price floors compared to the 2010s. Several dynamics merit monitoring:</p>
            <ul style="margin: 15px 0 15px 20px;">
                <li><strong>Energy transition pressures:</strong> As renewable energy adoption accelerates, long-term demand uncertainty has increased.</li>
                <li><strong>Supply discipline:</strong> OPEC+ and US shale producers have demonstrated willingness to manage supply, supporting price floors.</li>
                <li><strong>Macroeconomic sensitivity:</strong> Oil prices remain highly correlated with global growth expectations and monetary policy.</li>
            </ul>
            <p>For analysts contextualizing future market movements, the 56-year dataset provides a rich historical baseline—demonstrating that while prices are inherently unpredictable, the fundamental drivers of supply, demand, and geopolitics remain the persistent forces shaping the market.</p>
        </section>
